WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.187
OT disruption can produce consequences far beyond an unavailable application.

00:00:05.187 --> 00:00:13.532
Downtime may stop production, damage material, delay shipments, interrupt public service, or require expensive restart and cleanup.

00:00:13.532 --> 00:00:19.621
The financial impact can increase with every hour the process remains unstable.

00:00:19.621 --> 00:00:23.907
Safety and environmental consequences may dominate the decision.

00:00:23.907 --> 00:00:33.153
Loss of control, alarms, ventilation, cooling, or utility support can force a shutdown even when equipment still appears operational.

00:00:33.153 --> 00:00:39.243
Continuity planning defines conservative thresholds before teams face those choices under pressure.

00:00:39.243 --> 00:00:42.626
Supply chains can amplify local disruption.

00:00:42.626 --> 00:00:50.520
A single unavailable plant, terminal, treatment facility, or distribution node may affect customers and downstream operations.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:55.707
Contractual commitments, regulatory reporting, and public trust add further impact.

00:00:55.707 --> 00:00:59.541
Cyber incidents are one cause among many.

00:00:59.541 --> 00:01:06.984
Ransomware, destructive activity, credential compromise, or loss of remote access may interrupt visibility and control.

00:01:06.984 --> 00:01:15.779
Equipment failure, fire, flood, severe weather, utility loss, communications outages, and unavailable vendors can create similar continuity demands.

00:01:15.779 --> 00:01:17.809
Events can combine.

00:01:17.809 --> 00:01:22.545
A natural hazard may reduce staffing while damaging communications.

00:01:22.545 --> 00:01:29.086
A cyber incident may occur during planned maintenance when redundancy is already limited.

00:01:29.086 --> 00:01:36.529
Plans should avoid assuming that every control, person, supplier, and backup remains available at once.

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:39.009
Continuity investment reduces improvisation.

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:46.001
It identifies priorities, alternate modes, recovery resources, decision authority, and communication before an incident.

00:01:46.001 --> 00:01:58.856
The value is not a promise of zero downtime; it is the ability to protect people and the mission while making recovery faster, safer, and more predictable.
